According to a Colorado Supreme Court ruling, Donald Trump violated the Constitution in connection with his role in the uprising during the January 6, 2021 attack on the Capitol by his supporters. This is the first time a U.S. court has ruled in favor of the third section of the 14th Amendment to the US Constitution since being ratified in 1869 after the end of the Civil War.
What will happen next? The Colorado Supreme Court has decided to postpone the ruling until January 4, 2024, and that the issue should be resolved before January 5, 2024 is the day when each state issues a ballot for the primary election to select a presidential candidate.
The Colorado Supreme Court ruling will be finalized by the US Supreme Court, but it is not yet clear what the court will decide. But the US Supreme Court today has six conservative judges, including three conservative judges appointed by Trump, while only three liberal judges appointed by the president from the Democratic Party.
