Annual Meeting on Climate Change 2023 Reveals Cambodia's Commitment to a Sustainable Future
(Phnom Penh): The report of the Annual Meeting on Cambodia Climate Change 2023 (CCCS23) was released after the largest and most diverse annual meeting on climate change in Cambodia. The meeting provided important information on cross-sectoral action plans related to climate change in the country.
Twenty-five topics on climate change are covered in this report, as well as key information from the discussions of more than 90 experts from the public, private and other sectors.
This year's report shows that the new administration of the Ministry of Environment under the leadership of Eang Sophalleth has shown strong commitment to continuing the green diplomatic mission, reducing climate change and promoting a clean energy future.
This report focuses on topics such as climate financing, renewable energy, entrepreneurship in the renewable energy sector, solutions based on nature, water security, innovation in agriculture, sustainable tourism, sustainable solutions, green business, energy efficiency, carbon plus programs. REDD +) and compliance with regulations.
As an official partner of this year's CCCS23, Australia has played a key role in supporting the meeting through its largest economic development program, the Cambodia-Australia Partnership for Sustainable Economic Development (CAPRED). ).
Andreas Zurbrugg, Chargé d'Affaires of Australia for Cambodia, said: “This meeting provided an important and lively forum for participants with the participation of researchers and research institutions, leading thinkers, decision makers and implementers. "Projects from around the country are working together to address key issues related to climate change."
Additional support came from the United States Agency for International Development, a founding partner, and the Swiss Agency for Development and Cooperation, a major sponsor, Gold Level, the organizer of the Sustainable Solutions Expo. This is the first meeting that allows all participants to interact with leading businesses and organizations on project implementation on innovative climate change issues.
Minister of Environment Eang Sophalleth praised the importance of the event, emphasizing the impact of climate change and the catastrophe that could occur if no immediate action is taken.
MFI President Sear Rithy said the summit emphasized that a common understanding of tackling climate change is inseparable from the future of children, the nation and the world.
