Appeals Unit Remains a Problem in WTO Reform Plan
GENEVA: Members of the World Trade Organization (WTO), including India and China, on Monday expressed concern that a series of proposed reforms to the organization did not mention a reopening article. Of its appeals unit. According to Reuters, citing sources familiar with the matter.
WTO member states are reviewing the draft proposal, which is considered one of the strongest efforts to date, to reform this one-part system since December 2019. But the United States is blocking the transfer of judges to the organization's appeals body.
The same source told Reuters that the latest draft still has no plans for the restructuring of the appellate body, despite most countries trying to reach an agreement at a ministerial conference in Abu Dhabi last month. This coming February 2024.
A source monitoring Monday's private meeting in Geneva said India, China and South Africa were the countries that expressed concern and told Reuters: "Some members have expressed concern that "There is nothing about the appeals body in the edited article."
Countries aim to address the appeal system to allow billions of dollars worth of trade disputes to be resolved. Currently, the disputed country can still file a complaint to the WTO's lower arbitral tribunal, but if they do not accept the outcome of the lower level settlement, the case will be stuck at the appellate body. 30 has not been resolved yet.
"In resolving the issue of the appellate body, the delegation is actively seeking potential solutions that can reconcile the diverse views raised by member countries," he said.
The United States, which has criticized the former institution, appealed for its inefficiency and overwork, saying innovation was needed for the institution to make further progress.
"While we look forward, we must recognize that there is a lot of work to be done and that achieving fundamental conflict resolution reform will not be easy," a US official was quoted as saying by Reuters. .
US adds 13 Chinese companies to export control list
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The U.S. government said Tuesday it has added 13 more companies in China to its list of companies buying from the United States that US officials could not afford to inspect. According to Reuters.
A company is placed on a list that controls exports, or so-called "unverified lists", when U.S. export inspectors are unable to perform on-site inspections to determine whether Can they be trusted to acquire technology originating from the United States and other goods? Inspections of Chinese companies by the United States require the approval of the Chinese Ministry of Commerce.
Under a policy implemented during the administration of President Joe Biden, if a foreign government prevents US officials from conducting on-site inspections of companies on unverified lists, Washington will begin operations. Add the company to the Entity list 60 days later.
The entity list is a more stringent export management list than the unverified list. The United States will remove any companies from the list of unverified ones after successful inspections.
