(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Commerce has confirmed the action in the case of some foreign companies using the image of "Bayon and Angkor Wat" to package products without permission. This is according to the press release on the use of national symbols and national symbols on the packaging of products sold on the international market without permission, obtained by Fresh News on December 20, 2023.
According to the Ministry of Commerce, in the past (2020 and 2021), the Ministry of Commerce has cooperated with the Cambodian Embassy to prevent and successfully solve cases of fraudulent use of national symbols, flags and national symbols on products. Sold on the international market by a number of companies and individuals in violation of national law and international treaties.
At the same time, through the Overseas Trade Attorney, the Ministry of Commerce has issued directives and directs to companies and stores to remove illegal logos from their products and prohibit the continued use of trademarks. The national flag and the Cambodian national symbol on the packaging of the product as well.
In particular, the Ministry recently observed the use of trademarks on the packaging of Bayon and Angkor Wat temples on the packaging of noodles, sprayed rice and some other items on sale. Marketed in stores and online markets in California and other states in the United States without the permission of the Cambodian authorities.
The Ministry of Commerce would like to thank all the public in advance for providing information related to similar cases to the Ministry of Commerce. The Ministry has also been instructing the Deputy Attorney General and representatives of the Overseas Chamber of Commerce to continue their research and provide information to the Ministry of Commerce to take legal action on other cases.
The Ministry would like to thank the kindness and good cooperation of the people, both inside and outside the country, who have taken care to participate in protecting the culture, traditions and national wealth and promoting the reputation and reputation of our country. For more information and constructive feedback, please contact: 012 816 663, 012 387 878, 012 666 975.
