Stakeholders consult on the policy framework to promote the export of small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs)
(Phnom Penh): In a joint effort to improve Cambodia's business environment and promote cross-border e-commerce, the Ministry of Commerce in collaboration with Swisscontact on December 20, 2023 organized a consultation. On the policy framework with the private sector on the export of small packages to promote the export of small packages of small and medium enterprises to international markets.
Rajiv Prathan, Country Director of Swiss Contact, said that small and medium enterprises in Cambodia are the backbone of the economy, accounting for 98% of business and contributing more than 73% to job creation and 58% to GDP. . However, Cambodia's small and medium enterprises will contribute only 10% of Cambodia's exports, equivalent to about $ 1.6 thousand in 2020.
However, he noted that SMEs in some ASEAN countries accounted for 30% of exports, including Malaysia (28.1%), Thailand (34.7%) and the Philippines (33.4%). The data indicates that if small and medium enterprises in Cambodia get better support to boost exports to those countries, it will bring in an additional $ 3 billion for export markets.
"We all know that Cambodia has been promoting e-commerce not only locally but also internationally, with all key stakeholders working together to promote and "Our Cambodian product market is overseas, so it will contribute to increasing our exports."
Through today's workshop, she encourages small and medium enterprise owners to discuss and make suggestions to the Ministry of Commerce and other ministries and institutions, as well as stakeholders, to contribute to the development and implementation of policy frameworks for Improve the business environment and promote cross-border e-commerce effectively, transparently and in line with the real situation in Cambodia.
