(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Japan, through the Embassy in Cambodia, has agreed to provide more than 1.3 million US dollars to Cambodia to clear cluster munitions, landmines and unexploded ordnance.
The grant agreement under the Grant Scheme for the Japanese NGO project was signed on December 19, 2023 by the Japanese Ambassador to Cambodia, Ou Noo Atsushi, and a representative of the recipient body.
Recipient: Japan Mine Action Organization (JMAS) In this project, JMAS will clear 470 hectares of cluster munitions and train the Cambodian Mine Action Center (CMAC) on effective and safe methods of cluster munitions clearance. . In addition, the project will provide mine risk education to local people and train farmers on agricultural management and technology.
Recipient: JMAS In this project, JMAS will clear 180 hectares of landmines and unexploded ordnance and transfer effective and safe methods to clear landmines and unexploded ordnance to CMAC. The project will also provide mine risk education. In addition, farmers will have the opportunity to learn about rice cultivation techniques and set up model farms on demined land.
The Japanese ambassador said that the aid was allocated for a project to support reconstruction with cluster munitions clearance in Stung Treng province, known as the third year RSCS project, with a budget of nearly $ 700,000 implemented by the Japan Mine Action Organization. (JMAS).
He said that nearly $ 700,000 will be used to support the project to rebuild mines and unexploded ordnance in Banteay Meanchey province, also known as the third year RSDB project of JMAS on 180 hectares and transfer method. Effective and safe to clear mines and unexploded ordnance to CMAC.
The project will also provide mine risk education. In addition, farmers will have the opportunity to learn about rice cultivation techniques and set up model farms on demined land.
According to a report by the Embassy of Japan, a grant for a Japanese NGO project began in Cambodia in 2002 to support the activities of Japanese NGOs in their efforts to help develop and rebuild Cambodia. Back at the basic level.
Since then, the grant has provided Cambodia with a total of more than $ 52 million for 154 projects focusing on primary education, health, agriculture and demining.
