General knowledge: Where did agriculture first start and when?
(Phnom Penh): Agriculture has been an important source of livelihood for people since ancient times, it has been developed in different regions around the world at different times. Do you know where agriculture first started and when?
Agriculture first began about 13,000-10,000 years ago, when people in the Middle East began to grow grains and other fruit plants for daily living. They also started raising goats and sheep for meat and fur until they were able to graze animals for traction in agriculture.
Until about 11,000 years ago, people in India began farming in a form similar to Mesopotamia in the Middle East.
It also spread to the people of Egypt about 10,000 years ago. At the same time, people in South America, especially in present-day Mexico, are also learning to grow seeds and fruits for food.
Agriculture in ancient China began about 7,700 years ago. It was not until about 3,500-4,000 years ago that the ancient Chinese people began to make their first aquaculture, raising fish and growing water-dependent plants.
Until about 6,000 years ago, agriculture continued to spread to Europe, especially in the ancient Greek world.
In Southeast Asia, however, people began to grow rice to cook for daily consumption, which occurred around 5500 BC.
Over time, the people of these areas evolved from small-scale farming to large-scale farming, establishing farms and raising livestock to feed other families or to exchange for other things. Come back.
Farming, of course, paved the way for the development of modern society. Prehistoric people began to produce enough food to supply large villages, and those large farm villages later developed into the first cities, pre-monarchy in our world.
