(Phnom Penh): Komar Sith, son of Samdech Thipadei Hun Manet, Prime Minister of Cambodia, brought the gold medal from the math exam to show to Samdech Techo Hun Sen while he was exercising on the afternoon of December 16.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen posted a message on social media: "Sitting sweating after riding a bicycle, Chao Sith brought the gold medal from the math exam to show. A few years ago, Srey Mas, Chao Sith's older brother, brought the same ancestor. As a grandfather, he must be happy and consider that this is a real wealth that he distributes to his grandchildren who do not run out and do not dry up.
Hun Pech Monirithsith was born on the morning of October 9, 2017 and became the 20th grandson of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian People's Party and Chairman of the King's Personal Advisory Group.
Sri Lanka's economy returns to growth in Q3, led by agriculture
Sri Lanka's economy grew 1.6 percent in the second quarter as the country struggled to get out of its worst financial crisis in more than seven decades, official data showed on Friday. After a sharp drop in foreign exchange reserves ever. According to Reuters.
Reuters reports, citing a statement released by Sri Lanka's Census and Statistics Department, that this is the first growth since the end of 2021, driven by a relatively low base, lowering inflation. Rising currencies and lower interest rates.
Sri Lanka's agricultural sector grew 3 percent from a year earlier, while industrial output rose 0.3 percent while services rose 1.3 percent, according to the Census and Statistics Department.
With $ 2.9 billion in aid in March provided by the International Monetary Fund (IMF), Sri Lanka's economy has embarked on a painful path to growth. The country received a second bailout of the $ 2.9 billion bailout of $ 337 million last week, despite the IMF warning the island nation not to leave the crisis.
Sri Lanka's economy is expected to shrink 3.6 percent this year after declining 7.8 percent in 2022, according to the IMF.
Full-year growth will return next year, with the country's economy projected to grow 1.8 percent, but reform challenges remain ahead, such as higher taxes, reforms to state-owned enterprises Losses and full debt restructuring for foreign debt. According to Reuters.
