The Ministry of Agriculture, in collaboration with the Cambodian Agro-Ecological Network, urges farmers to change their old farming practices to new ones that are more profitable.
Mr. Om Kimsa, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries and Chairman of the Kasik Orientation Committee, pointed out that there are currently a small number of farmers in Cambodia who practice agro-ecological farming, which is why experts advise farmers. Change the old way of farming to the new way of farming so that farmers can profit from this business.
"We are trying to compare the facts, especially in terms of economic efficiency," he added. "Until we see that economic efficiency is better than traditional farming, we can reach out to him. If economic efficiency is weaker than traditional farming, we do not dare to start."
According to a senior official of the Ministry of Agriculture, Kasik was established in 2020 to support agro-ecological agriculture based on three principles: 1. Farming by reducing land disturbance through mitigation. Plowing or not making openings and soil erosion. Second. Planting cover crops to retain water in the soil, increase fertilizer and be a habitat for biodiversity; and Alternate cropping.
Currently, there are farmers in about 10 provinces who participate in agro-ecological farming in Battambang, Kandal, Kampong Speu, Preah Vihear, Siem Reap, Kampong Thom, Kratie, Tbong Khmum and Mondulkiri.
Chan Saruth, undersecretary of state at the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries and chairman of the Kasik Executive Committee, said that according to the results of the annual survey, Cambodia faces an estimated loss of $ 600 million to $ 700 million due to landslides. Causes loss of nutrients. He said that eco-agriculture is a good solution, which is why he encourages farmers across the country to turn to eco-agriculture.
"We understand that with this technique we can help the farmers to see him participate, but the decision is important," he said. "We are not the ones who decide for him, but if he sees that doing so, he is making a profit and his land is better, he will do it."
