MEXICO CITY - The International Rescue Committee (IRC) said in a report on Thursday that climate change, armed conflict, worsening debt burden, as well as shrinking international support will exacerbate the global humanitarian crisis. Serious in 2024. This is according to the release by Reuters on Thursday, December 14, 2023.
In its 2024 Emergency Situation Report, the New York-based IRC identified 20 countries, most of them in Africa, as having the highest risk of a worsening humanitarian situation by 2024. The report comes after the number of people in need of humanitarian assistance in 2023 reached 300 million, while the number of people forced to flee increased to 110 million.
Sudan tops the IRC Emergency Situation Report, followed by Palestine and South Sudan. Under them are nine countries south of the Sahara, while in Asia there are Myanmar, Afghanistan, in the Middle East, Syria, Lebanon and Yemen, in Europe there is Ukraine, in the Caribbean there is South America, Ecuador and Haiti. .
These 20 territories account for 10% of the world's population, but account for 86% of global humanitarian needs, as well as 70% of the displaced population, and an increasing share of those at risk of poverty and risk. Extreme weather. Photo by Reuters Kampong Thom Provincial Department of Social Affairs distributes donations to 200 vulnerable families in Baray district
