General knowledge: Discover the history of Taiwan, a province of China
(Phnom Penh): Taiwan is an island located in the southeast of the People's Republic of China. As of 2018, the island has a population of more than 23 million and has a political regime leading the country and even declared itself an independent territory.
Although Taiwan has so far been claimed by the ruling Kuomintang party as the legitimate state of China as a whole, the 36,000-square-kilometer area is just a province. Only one of the Chinese emperors. This is according to the Constitution of the People's Republic of China.
First of all, one has to find out from the time of the fall of the monarchy in China:
In other words, before former General Yeon Sikai took office as President of the Republic, Sun Yat-sen had already announced the establishment of the Republic. At that time, on January 1, 1912, a revolutionary group led by Sun Yat-sen announced the establishment of the People's Republic of China, but he had not yet succeeded in overthrowing the monarchy.
We will find out why they dare to claim this:
The Kuomintang, founded by Sun Yat-sen, remains the ruling party in Taiwan to this day. The Kuomintang, or Chinese Nationalist Party, was formed on August 24, 1912.
The reason for forming this party was because Sun Yat-sen wanted to rally against President Yingluck Shinawatra, because when he resigned from the presidency, it was a forced surrender caused by a weak military force. In December 1912, China held the first and smooth parliamentary elections, and then Sun Yat-sen's party won a landslide victory among all Chinese political parties, including the ruling party of Yan Xi. Khai, too.
However, Yeon Sikai disagreed with the election results and planned to assassinate a senior Kuomintang official who was scheduled to become prime minister. The senior official, Song Soo-sen, was shot dead while waiting for a train to travel to Beijing. The assassination, which has been linked to a series of assassinations, has sparked controversy between supporters of the Kuomintang party and Yen Sikai's party.
Conflicts between the two major Chinese political parties after the election led to a second revolution by Sun Yat-sen, who had succeeded in overthrowing the monarchy, with the aim of overthrowing the government of Yen Sikai. Unfortunately, with so little troops, the revolution was completely defeated and Sun Yat-sen even fled to Japan.
The defeat of Sun Yat-sen caused Yeon Sikai to gather all his power alone without anyone daring to rise up against him. President Yen Sikai did not know how to lead a republic, and four years after the fall of the monarchy, in December 1915, Yen Sikai decided to re-establish the regime and establish himself as emperor.
It should be noted that the name of the emperor who gave his own name, Yeon Sikai, was not respected and obeyed. Less than a year later, on March 22, 1916, Yue Xikai dissolved the monarchy and abdicated as emperor, leading China as president.
On June 6, 1916, Yeon Sikai died of an illness. After his death, his former commander took his subordinates to serve them and lived like their respective warlords.
For more than 10 years after the death of Yuan Sikai, China had no real leader, as the central government ruled only in Beijing, while other areas were ruled by independent powers. According to the assessment, the Chinese people at that time were very miserable.
Back in the day, Sun Yat-sen's Kuomintang Nationalist Party shook hands with the Chinese Communist Party, despite differences between the two groups. The communists wanted to establish a communist state and abolish the feudal class left over from the monarchy, but the Kuomintang focused on cracking down on warlords to unite the nation and lead the country to industrial growth.
Sun Yat-sen also has military cooperation with Zhang Kai-shek, director of a military training school in Guangdong Province, China. After Sun Yat-sen died of liver cancer in 1925, the position of the Chinese Nationalist Party was transferred to Zhang Kai-shek.
Zhang Kai-hek did not like the communists. In mid-1926, the director of the military academy launched a large-scale military operation and seized territory once occupied by warlords and overthrew the government in Beijing.
The communist movement under President Mao Zedong, restructured in the style of Marxism-Leninism, has made remarkable progress. Mao Zedong was a powerful leader who learned the military leadership strategies of the ancient Chinese scholar Sun Su and loved to read the story of Sam Kok, devised a powerful military leadership strategy, the guerrilla strategy.
Later, during the Japanese invasion of China, Zhang Kai-hek did not care much because he felt that defeating Mao Zedong's group was more important. This displeased the Chinese people and even accused Zhang Kai-hek of being a traitor who sold land to Japan, while Mao Zedong prioritized anti-Japanese sentiment. Strong.
In 1939, due to the outbreak of World War II due to both the civil war and the Japanese invasion, mainland China was divided into three parts. The eastern part was occupied by the Japanese, the western and southern parts were occupied by Zhang Kai-shek, while the north-western part (north-west) was occupied by the Mao Zedong Communist Party.
During World War II, Mao Zedong had more than 1 million troops and controlled a territory of more than 100 million people. Looking at Zhang Kai-shek, who received US aid, there were a large number of troops, but the quality was poor.
Chang Kai-shek's generals were appointed and promoted through partisanship, regardless of ability, and corruption was rampant in the army. Most of the generals inflated the number of soldiers and took over the military regime, leaving the soldiers on the battlefield short of food and weapons.
Zhang Kaiqiang lost the war in disgrace and fled to Taiwan:
After World War II, although the United States disliked Zhang Kai-shek's leadership, it continued to provide billions of dollars in military aid to the administration in its fight against Mao Zedong. The United States did not want China to fall under Mao Zedong because Mao Zedong also received aid from the Soviet Union.
Zhang Kai-shek's Kuomintang forces were four times larger than Mao Zedong's Communist Party forces. The government forces numbered more than 4 million, while the Mao Zedong forces, then known as the Rebels, numbered just over 1 million. Defeated Mao Zedong in succession.
In 1948, Mao Zedong's forces seized Manchuria and captured more than 400,000 government soldiers. Mao Zedong soon besieged the area of more than 400,000 government troops, and at that time, fearing that Mao Zedong would take over the area, Zhang Kai-hek intended to use fighter jets to drop bombs to destroy the area, but his troops also learned this. They dropped their weapons and peacefully surrendered to Mao Zedong.
This increased the balance of forces toward Mao Zedong. On the other hand, Zhang Kai-shek has lost both public trust in his inability to lead the country and the country's economic problems due to rising commodity prices and declining paper prices.
In 1949, Mao Zedong's army invaded Nanjing, the administrative region of Zhang Kai-shek, forcing the government to flee to Guangdong and Sichuan, eventually fleeing to Taiwan. Zhang Kaiqiang brought in nearly 2 million officials and soldiers, as well as relatives, and declared Taipei, Taiwan, the temporary capital of the People's Republic of China.
The whole of mainland China came under the control of Mao Zedong's Chinese Communist Party, and he reorganized China. In addition, on October 1, 1949, President Mao Zedong proclaimed the founding of the People's Republic of China, with Beijing as its capital ever since.
