China lifts restrictions on meat imports from three Australian slaughterhouses (Video inside) Meat from three slaughterhouses in Australia, the latest sign of improved relations between the two countries. According to Reuters.
China has barred Australian imports of goods, including coal, timber and sorghum, after Australia called for an investigation into the source of the Kovid-19 virus by 2020. Restrictions were imposed on three slaughterhouses in 2020 and 2022 following Kovid outbreaks among the slaughterhouse workers.
Most of these restrictions have been lifted since a change of government in Canberra last year. China is Australia's largest overseas buyer of lamb and the fourth largest buyer of beef, with last year importing more than $ 1 billion worth of lamb and $ 270 million worth of beef.
While other commodity trade has collapsed due to tariffs or other trade barriers, many Australian slaughterhouses are still able to export meat to China, and export volumes have fluctuated little in recent years. .
The Australian Ministry of Commerce and the Ministry of Agriculture have said that China's customs agency has announced the lifting of export restrictions.
