Global trade to fall 5% this year amid geopolitical crisis
(Geneva) - The United Nations Trade and Development Conference (UNCTAD) says global trade will shrink by about 5% in 2023 from last year's highest growth due to higher borrowing costs, burdening the economy. Tensions between the United States and China have shifted supply chains and other policies that have restricted cross-border trade.
The value of trade in goods and services will reach $ 30.7 trillion, compared with $ 32.2 trillion, Bloomberg reported, citing a report released by Geneva-based UNCTAD on Monday. Million dollars in 2022. The $ 2 trillion drop in trade in goods, or 8% of trade in goods, was the main reason for the slowdown in world trade.
The report said the trade in services this year will reach $ 500 billion, or about 7% from last year.
Lower costs for goods affected by last year's high inflation were also a reason for declining trade. "Although commodity prices fell in 2023, the less positive trend in international trade volume indicates a global demand for resilient imports," the report said.
UNCTAD has noted how countries with geopolitical consistency are doing more trade with each other, while other countries that are at odds are doing less bilateral trade. The UN agency said differences among many issues were hurting the trade outlook for next year.
"Forecasts for world trade remain highly uncertain and generally pessimistic," the report said. "While some economic indicators show potential improvement, chronic geopolitical tensions, high debt levels and widespread economic fragility are expected to have a negative impact on the model," the report added. Global trade.
Saudi Arabia discusses expanding trade, investment and technology cooperation with China
BEIJING (Reuters) - Saudi Arabia's investment minister met with Chinese Minister of Commerce and Industry in Beijing on Sunday to discuss expanding cooperation in trade, investment and technology. According to Reuters.
"China is willing to work with Saudi Arabia to jointly promote China's infrastructure, belt and road investment programs and initiatives," Commerce Minister Wang Wentao was quoted as saying by Reuters. 2030 "of Saudi Arabia.
The "Vision 2030" initiative aims to diversify the economy of Saudi Arabia, the world's largest oil producer, away from hydrocarbon-dependent revenues, and to include major infrastructure projects and focus. On the private sector.
According to the Ministry of Industry and Information Technology, Jin said China is open to working with Saudi Arabia on new energy vehicles, aircraft, solar technology (photovoltaics) and artificial intelligence technology, as well as cooperation to protect the industrial supply chain. Global, too.
Relations between China and Saudi Arabia have deepened recently, with Saudi Arabia's energy minister announcing in June that his country would seek cooperation with China, not compete with each other. According to Reuters.
