Indonesia vows to tackle Rohingya humanitarian crisis amid rising inflows
JAKARTA: Indonesia will continue to deal with the influx of humanitarian Rohingya refugees after the latest ethnic minority group arrived in Aceh province, the Indonesian National Refugee Team said on Sunday.
The westernmost part of the country, amid growing opposition from locals. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on December 10, 2023.
More than 1,200 Rohingya refugees have arrived in Aceh since mid-November but have faced opposition from local authorities and residents who have threatened to push them back to the sea. The Indonesian Refugee Team says the safety of the Rohingya is a top priority for the government.
Most Rohingya Muslims have suffered decades of persecution in Myanmar and have been described as "the most persecuted minority in the world" by the Coalition. Nation.
In 2017, more than 730,000 Rohingya fled to neighboring Bangladesh after a brutal crackdown by the Myanmar military, which the United Nations has called genocide. For the last six years, refugees have been living in overcrowded camps in Cox's Bazar, Bangladesh, where humanitarian aid has plummeted and insecurity has risen.
