Singapore signs carbon credit agreement with Papua New Guinea
The agreement comes after the two countries signed a memorandum of understanding during COP27 last year.
The Ministry of Commerce and Industry (MTI), the Ministry of Sustainability and Environment (MSE) and the National Climate Change Secretariat (NCCS) of Singapore said in a joint statement that the agreement is also the first implementation agreement. Also for Singapore.
The agreement sets out the framework and process for binding legal obligations for the establishment and transfer of international carbon credits between Singapore and Papua New Guinea.
"The cooperation will boost the two countries' climate ambitions and help finance further mitigation efforts through carbon credit schemes without this agreement," the two agencies said. "It will not be possible."
The two agencies added: "The carbon credit projects authorized under the implementation agreement will also promote sustainable development and benefit local communities, such as job creation, access to clean water, improved energy security and sustainable development." Reduce pollution as well. ”Real estate experts say the last four years of global crisis have taught business owners to be patient and manage their finances properly.
