China says plans to cut off US electric vehicle battery supply chains violate WTO rules (Video inside) (BEIJING) China, which uses electric car batteries to qualify for US tax credits from next year, violates international trade standards and will disrupt global supply chains.
Reuters reports that the plan would disqualify investors in the US electric vehicle (EV) supply chain from receiving tax credits if they exceeded the limit on key raw materials from China. Or other countries that are considered to be foreign countries or entities that cause concern (FEOC).
China's prominent role in the global car battery supply chain has prompted US and European officials to take action over fears that China's low-cost electric vehicle (EV) could flood their markets.
The European Commission is currently investigating whether Chinese automakers are unfairly benefiting from state subsidies.
Washington has already passed two laws, excluding investors from benefiting from a $ 6 billion tax credit for essential batteries and minerals and a $ 7,500 subsidy. For every new energy vehicle produced if those investors use raw materials imported from FEOCs for the production of their electric vehicles.
The administration of US President Joe Biden is also proposing stricter criteria, including determining whether a company with a 25 percent stake is owned by one of the FEOC countries is a state-owned company. Or not.
China has nearly two-thirds of the world's lithium recycling capacity and 75 percent of the cobalt processing capacity used to make batteries. According to Reuters.
