Government Spokesperson: Withdrawal of one or two illegal media licenses does not affect the freedom of the press in Cambodia, which has more than 2,000 institutions (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Mr. Pen Bona, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Leader The Royal Government has stated that the revocation of the licenses of one or two illegal media organizations does not affect the freedom of the press in Cambodia, which has more than 2,000 institutions.
Mr. Pen Bona's statement was made on December 7, 2023 at a roundtable discussion on "Freedom of Expression and Accountability" after saying that as Human Rights Day approaches On December 10, there will be criticism of the decline in press freedom in Cambodia after the closure of VOD radio.
With this statement, Mr. Pen Bona even denied the nickname of VOD Radio, the last independent news institution that was shut down by the Royal Government of Cambodia.
Mr. Pen Bona stated, "Recently, they took up the case of revoking the license of 2 or 4 media, taking that matter to assess the situation of freedom of the press in Cambodia." I would like to inform the press that more than 2,000 media outlets with tens of thousands of journalists working in Cambodia have become hostages of one or two illegal media outlets and their licenses have been revoked to assess the overall situation in Cambodia. "In Cambodia, press freedom is declining."
He added that so far, Cambodia has more than 2,000 media outlets, tens of thousands of journalists and all kinds of opinions on Facebook, so the freedom of the press is not declining.
