Myanmar's central bank will no longer set the exchange rate for foreign currency and will allow banks and exchange companies to decide on their own interest rates, a rare easing, Myanmar state media reported on Wednesday. On its strict foreign exchange management. According to Reuters.
In the past, as Myanmar's economy has been in shambles since the 2021 coup and foreign exchange reserves are under pressure, authorities have put in place measures to curb demand for foreign currency amid crackdowns on currency trading. Overseas on the black market and revoked the exchange licenses of more than 140 locations this year.
Reuters reported that the report did not provide a reason for the central bank's decision made on Tuesday, and the central bank did not post a decision on the policy change on its website.
Under military rule, the central bank has shifted away from a regulated market exchange system and relied on administrative controls, including requiring companies to convert foreign currencies into local currencies and report trading. Currency.
Some exporters are required to convert dollar-denominated earnings into Myanmar kyat at the official exchange rate set by the central bank, which in August ordered local ministries and governments not to use foreign currency for domestic transactions. Loosen pressure on the dollar.
Sean Turnell, an Australian economist who was an adviser to the Myanmar government before the coup and was jailed along with some top leaders, was quoted by Reuters as saying the central bank's move was a sign that The military regime is in trouble.
