National Assembly President Khuon Sodary and Lao National Assembly President Nguyen Phu Trong vow to strengthen parliamentary cooperation and partnership to promote common interests of CLV people
In addition, the leaders of the Cambodian, Laotian and Vietnamese parliaments are jointly committed to promoting cooperation in the fields of politics, defense, security, culture and economy for socio-economic development, and pledged to play a monitoring role in addressing the remaining challenges. For the common good of the people living in the CLV-DTA area.
The first Cambodia-Laos-Vietnam Parliamentary Summit (CLV), hosted by the Lao National Assembly, was held from December 4 to 7, 2023 under the theme "Strengthening the Role of Parliament in Promoting "Comprehensive cooperation between Cambodia, Laos and Vietnam."
The first Cambodia-Laos-Vietnam Parliamentary Summit (CLV) is an upgrade of the Cambodian-Lao-Vietnamese Parliamentary Assembly from the Expert Committee level to the National Assembly leadership level in accordance with the agreement between the President of the National Assembly of Cambodia and Laos. Vietnam, which signed the agreement in 2022 to strengthen parliamentary cooperation, supports the CLV-DTA development triangle by deepening the three countries' parliamentary mechanisms.
At the first plenary session on December 5, National Assembly President Khuon Sodary said that strengthening the parliamentary mechanism to participate in the CLV-DTA development project now is a testament to its goal. Strategies based on mutual respect and trust.
The CLV Development Triangle was established in 1993 by the heads of government of Cambodia, Laos and Vietnam in 13 provinces, including Cambodia in four provinces: Ratkiri, Mondulkiri, Kratie and Stung Treng. Laos has four provinces and Vietnam has five.
The CLV Summit praised the achievements of the three governments in terms of legal, policy and development standards. During the two days, the meeting discussed and heard reports from ministries, institutions, relevant authorities from Cambodia, Laos and Vietnam.
