Moody's downgrades China's credit rating outlook over debt worries
China's post-epidemic Kovid-19 economic recovery has been hampered by declining consumer and business confidence, chronic housing crisis, high youth unemployment and slowing global economic growth. On the demand for Chinese goods.
The crisis has put pressure on central and local governments to take further financial support after Beijing issued 1 trillion yuan ($ 137 billion) in government bonds in October.
According to AFP on Tuesday, Moody's said its decision "reflects the growing evidence that financial support will be provided by the government and the wider public sector to local governments and governments." Local and state enterprises.
The debt rating agency said this "poses a significant downside risk to China's fiscal, economic and institutional strength."
The downgrade "reflects the increased risks associated with lower-than-medium-term, structural and chronic growth and the continued decline in the real estate sector."
China's massive real estate sector is mired in a deep debt crisis, with some of China's largest developers owed hundreds of billions of dollars and facing bankruptcy.
The Chinese Ministry of Finance responded that it was disappointed by Moody's decision.
A spokesman was quoted by AFP as saying: "Since the beginning of this year, we have faced a complex and serious international situation and resisted the volatile global economic recovery and the slowdown in the economy. "China's macroeconomic downturn continues to recover."
"Moody's concerns about China's economic growth prospects and fiscal sustainability are not necessary," the spokesman said.
