HANOI: The Vietnamese government will investigate the central bank's credit growth after the government complained that credit growth was too slow. According to Reuters.
Vietnam's economic growth is largely dependent on credit growth, controlled by the State Bank of Vietnam (SBV), the central bank of Vietnam, which sets annual targets for banks with the aim of regulating lending and controlling credit growth.
The central bank, which is targeting 14 percent credit growth for the banking system this year, said on Thursday that lending had risen just 8.21 percent as of Nov. 22 from a year earlier. Unbalanced credit among banks.
The Vietnamese government issued another statement, Khai told the central bank at a meeting last week that credit growth was too low, prompting the central bank to review the report, Reuters reported. Repeat lending activities of each commercial bank.
The central bank said credit growth was slow because "the economy is still struggling with a slow recovery and therefore weak demand for loans."
