LONDON (Reuters) - Sri Lanka's international bond holders said on Friday that they welcomed Sri Lanka's debt restructuring agreement with official creditors, despite the group saying there was a lack of transparency in the deal. The past is sad, though.
Reuters reports that Sri Lanka and its creditor nations, including Japan, France and India, on Wednesday reached an agreement in principle on repaying $ 5.9 billion in government debt owed. The deal follows an agreement between the island nation and the Export-Import Bank of China in October on a $ 4.2 billion loan.
But creditors, representing the country's $ 12 billion debtors, say a lack of transparency between government creditors and private creditors is making it harder for them to reach an agreement with Sri Lanka. Which is an agreement in accordance with the rules of the IMF and which provides for the treatment of debt "fair and equitable.
"The working group finds it unfortunate that there is still a lack of transparency on the part of the formal creditors," the working group on behalf of the bondholders said in an e-mailed statement. "Despite the team's efforts so far to act as a constructive counterpart."
