Dr. Eang Sophalleth Attends COP-28 UN Climate Change Summit in Dubai
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Eang Sophalleth, Minister of the Ministry of Environment and High Representative of the Prime Minister of Cambodia Samdech Hun Manet, is leading a Cambodian inter-ministerial delegation to attend the G77 + China Summit in the United Nations Framework Summit. On Climate Change COP-28 in Dubai.
At the 28th Summit, the Minister of Environment and all delegates prepared seven Cambodian gifts to be distributed to more than 50,000 participants, including:
3. Increase renewable energy, mostly solar energy, hydropower and wind energy to 70%.
4. A clean Cambodia strategy by reducing pollution by plastic bags and solids and liquids, with the participation of more than 3 million Cambodians and is growing.
5. Green Cambodia strategy by planting at least 1 million trees in both open and protected areas per year, expanding forest cover to 60% by 2050.
6- Continue not to allow the establishment of coal-fired power plants in Cambodia.
7. Continue to not allow the construction of hydropower across the Mekong River.
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training opened a fire safety training course for a total of 122 civil servants and inspectors of the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training at the Ministry of Labor on November 30, 2023 under the chairmanship of HE Dr. Huy. Han Song, Secretary of State and Representative of HE Heng Sour, Minister of Labor and Vocational Training.
On that occasion, Mr. Leng Tong, Director of the Department of Labor Medicine, said that the workshop was organized to disseminate and raise awareness on fire safety and fire risk prevention measures.
The director of the department said that in order to prevent fire hazards, the workplace needs to have a risk assessment of the fire by considering the components of the fire, have assigned staff in charge of fire safety, set up fire prevention and extinguishing systems. According to technical standards for prevention and regular inspection.
At the same time, Dr. Huy Hansong instructed the inspectors of the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training to inform the employers, managers in factories, enterprises and establishments about the law on fire safety system to ensure safety. Employees in their positions.
The doctor stressed that all necessary equipment must be classified and stored in the correct location according to its type, including means of firefighting in case of fire to be easy to use and extinguish in time. In addition, the employer must have a fire management team at all times.
This is the explanation of Mr. Rama, Technical Officer for Research and Development of Agro-Ecological and Food Safety System (ASSET) Development Project, who is explaining the different types of land: forest land, land that farmers normally cultivate and Soil cover crops to restore soil quality.
He said that in general, rice can take root only 15 to 20 cm into the soil, so rice can not extract nutrients in the deep soil layer, if farmers continue to farm normally, can not. Make the most of the land potential.
Heng Hour, a farmer in Bos Reak Reay village, Rovieng district, Preah Vihear province, said that for more than four years, since the end of 2019, he has started planting cover crops to restore quality and increase rice yields.
Dr. Kong Rada, agro-ecologist and national coordinator of the Agro-Ecological Development and Food Safety System (ASSET) project, said there are many types of ground cover crops, such as legumes and grasses, and their use depends on the species. Of cultivating various crops. Apparently, for rice, the most commonly used ground cover crop is Stilo, a small-leaved monkey.
He said that in addition to rice, farmers who grow cassava or cashews can also grow cover crops. He said that cassava is a crop that absorbs and needs more nutrients from the soil compared to other crops, so the quality of cassava fields from year to year is easy to decline and yields are also reduced. .
He said that with the support of the ASSET project to support farmers in Rovieng district, Preah Vihear province, the cultivation of land cover can also become a new business for farmers by planting cover crops and harvesting seeds. For sale.
According to Kong Rada, the current price of stucco seeds that farmers grow and sell to the community is 25,000 riel per kilogram. Separately, the resale community costs 32,000 riels per kilogram, while in the market where private companies put up for sale is worth 400,000 riels.
Sam Bunthoeun, a farmer in Rovieng district, Preah Vihear province, who has been planting stucco cover on 25 acres for two years, said that last year she harvested about 23 kilograms of stucco seeds and sold them to the community and other farmers. Another received a budget of more than 500,000 riels, with a price of 25,000 riels per kilogram. As for the stems, she used them as fertilizer for the fields.
