US tells Israel that any future military action in Gaza must avoid further civilian displacement
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The Biden administration has told Israel it must avoid "more serious displacements" of Palestinian civilians in the southern Gaza Strip, a senior US official said Tuesday. If the country continues its ground military operations aimed at defeating Hamas militants in the future. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on November 28, 2023.
The United States is seeking to avoid further large-scale civilian deaths or large-scale displacement, as seen before the current ceasefire.
Amid rising international and domestic pressure over the Palestinian death toll, the White House has begun to increase pressure on Israel to "carefully consider" the approach to future military operations, an official said. "When government officials raised these concerns, Israel easily accepted them," he added.
Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu has made it clear that the Israeli Defense Forces will eventually resume military operations after the current ceasefire is ended. The two sides agreed on Monday to extend the ceasefire for another two days and to exchange more hostages and prisoners.
