The race to develop artificial intelligence has intensified since the launch of the ChatGPT system last year, with Russia and China spending billions to challenge US dominance in the sector.
"I think you know very well that some Western search engines, as well as some general models, always work in a selective and biased way," Putin said at a conference on artificial intelligence in Moscow.
"They do not care about Russian culture and sometimes they just ignore it and cancel it ... Many modern systems are trained on Western data for their market," he added. The Russian president stressed that the monopoly of such foreign innovations in Russia is "dangerous" and unacceptable.
Russia's tech industry has struggled after Moscow invaded Ukraine. Thousands of IT workers have fled Kenya, while Western sanctions have also blocked the import of some key raw materials.
Putin has repeatedly called on Moscow to stop relying on Western technology and last September ordered the government to invest in the development of modern computers and artificial intelligence research.
