The following is a press release from the Ministry of Rural Development: Five Indian soldiers and two militants were killed in fighting in Kashmir.
Kashmir: Five soldiers and two suspected rebels were killed in Indian-administered Kashmir during a shootout in the disputed Himalayas, a Han official said on Thursday. This November 23, published by the AFP news agency.
A "fierce battle" erupted on Wednesday after the army chased the militants into the dense Calakot forest of the south, 16 army units said in a post on social media. Kashmir region.
An army official, speaking on condition of anonymity, said the fighting left five soldiers dead, including two Special Forces officers and wounded another. The military did not provide details on the number. Suspected rebels involved.
Kashmir has been divided between India and Pakistan since their independence in 1947, with each claiming the entire plateau but controlling it separately.
Rebels have been waging insurgency in Indian-administered Kashmir since 1989, demanding independence or unification with Pakistan. The conflict has left tens of thousands of civilians, soldiers and militants dead.
India has blamed Pakistan for supporting the militants, which Islamabad denies, saying it only supports the Kashmir people's struggle for self-determination.
Indian authorities say at least 118 people have been killed in clashes this year, including 11 civilians, 27 security forces and 80 suspected rebels.
Permanent Deputy Prime Minister Vongsey Visoth chaired an inter-ministerial meeting to review and discuss the draft Royal Decree on the organization and functioning of the Council for the Development of Cambodia.
The inter-ministerial meeting reviewed and discussed the draft Royal Decree on the organization and functioning of the Council for the Development of Cambodia, which has eight chapters and 24 articles prepared by the Council for the Development of Cambodia.
Permanent Deputy Prime Minister Vongsey Visoth thanked and appreciated the representatives of ministries and institutions who participated in the discussion and provided good feedback to make the draft Royal Decree more efficient, effective and consistent and responsive. To work to promote the attraction and promotion of investment for socio-economic development of Cambodia.
(Banteay Meanchey): Poipet Water Drop Organization has established a school to help children who drop out of school and suffer from drug addiction, homeless children, dragging scooters to get education and open the enrollment campaign on November 23, 2023 This.
Mr. Long San Rithy, Director of Poipet Water Drop Organization, said that the development primary school was established by Poipet Water Drop Organization to train children who drop out of school to walk, pick up scrap metal, pull carts and out of school by Poipet Water Drop Organization. Go to school, become a good child, a good student and a successor of bamboo.
Mr. Ky Seng Heang, Deputy Director of Poipet City Education Office, praised Poipet Water Drop Organization for setting up a development primary school to train drop-out children to go to school. This is a contribution to the education sector to strengthen the quality of education in the border areas so that the fighters can get an education and stay away from drugs, gangsters, younger brothers and older brothers to come and study for the future of themselves and their families.
Suban, Deputy Director of Poipet City Administration, said that before 1999, Poipet City had vulnerable children who used poor drugs, begged for scrap metal, dragged carts, slept on the sidewalks and abandoned porches. Study.
With the attention of the Royal Administration of Cambodia, the Ministry of Specialization and the Banteay Meanchey Provincial Administration and the Poipet Municipality, in cooperation with the Poipet Water Drop Organization, have made it possible for all children to be rehabilitated and eliminated. Get an education, have knowledge and have a job for yourself and help your family.
