Cambodia, Japan Agree to Further Strengthen Multilateral Cooperation Under ASEAN-Japan Relations Framework and Mekong-Japan Cooperation
(Phnom Penh): Cambodia and Japan have agreed to further strengthen multilateral cooperation under the framework of ASEAN-Japan relations and Mekong-Japan cooperation. The two sides also exchanged broad views on regional and international issues of common concern and interest.
In addition, the two sides agreed that Cambodia-Japan relations have grown to a new level with the upgrading of bilateral relations to a comprehensive strategic partnership earlier this year. To further promote mutually beneficial cooperation in key areas, including: politics, economy, defense and security, digital development, people-to-people ties.
Both sides noted with satisfaction the frequent exchanges of high-level visits and communication between the two countries, especially the recent summit between Cambodian Prime Minister Hun Manet and Japanese Prime Minister Kishida Fimio. The summit has contributed to increasing mutual trust and further expanding bilateral ties.
Both sides expressed satisfaction with the current state of economic cooperation, especially the stability of bilateral trade and large Japanese investment in Cambodia. The two sides also discussed ways to boost bilateral trade and attract more Japanese investment to Cambodia.
On November 21, Ms. Tuot Panha met separately with Mr. HORI Iwao, First Deputy Minister of Foreign Affairs of Japan. The two sides had fruitful discussions on further strengthening good relations and multilateral cooperation under bilateral and multilateral frameworks.
The Cambodian side thanked the Japanese side for the warm welcome and the excellent organization of this first high-level consultation. The two sides agreed to hold the second High-Level Consultation in Cambodia in 2024 on a date suitable for both parties.
