(Palestine): The first ceasefire between Israel and Hamas has been agreed with the conditions for the release of some hostages and the release of more Palestinian prisoners. The first phase of the ceasefire will last four days, with the release of 50 hostages. The next phase of the ceasefire is said to be based on the one-time release of 10 hostages in exchange for a one-day ceasefire.
The announcement of the ceasefire came just hours after the Israeli cabinet approved the agreement. The United States and Egypt are among the countries that have contributed to the ceasefire talks and the release of these people.
The release of the hostages will begin within 24 hours after all parties agree to the terms of the ceasefire. Earlier, four hostages were released on humanitarian grounds, including two Americans and two Israelis.
Biden said the agreement should release some American hostages and that he would not stop until all the hostages were released.
An Israeli government statement said the ceasefire could be followed by a day-long ceasefire, requiring the release of 10 more hostages.
Prolonged ceasefires could also put pressure on the Israeli government, both domestically and internationally, because ending the war will not make Israel as successful as it would like to destroy Hamas' military capabilities. The International Committee of the Red Cross (ICRC) says it is preparing to help with these exchanges between Israel and Hamas.
The 47-day war began with a bloody incident in Israel on October 7, with Hamas launching a surprise attack across the border into Israel, killing at least 1,200 people. About 240 people were taken hostage.
