(Phnom Penh): The World Bank has approved a $ 163 million project to assist Cambodia in its efforts to strengthen water and sanitation services for the benefit of more than 175,000 people in four provinces. . This is according to the World Bank press release obtained by Fresh News on November 22, 2023.
"This support will help the government build a strong foundation for providing these essential services to the Cambodian people and will bring immediate benefits to women," said Maria Salim, World Bank Country Manager for Cambodia. And the rural poor who do not currently have access to these services. ”
The project has an activity to maximize financial investment for development by working with private water supply operators who are already contributing more than half of all pipelines in Cambodia in mobilizing approximately 17 resources. An additional $ 6 million from private sources to expand water supply.
Hem Vandy, Minister of Industry, Science, Technology and Innovation, said: "Supply of clean water is a priority for sustainable and sustainable development and for economic development. "This project will assist the government in implementing its vision and overall development strategy, and we are committed to ensuring sustainable growth in this area as well as achieving SDG 6 goals."
Minister of Public Works and Transport Peng Po Na said, "The Royal Government of Cambodia pays close attention to the management of wastewater and sewage sludge, so investment in sanitation under this project is very important." "To ensure the sustainability of sanitation services, which will make the sector a positive development in the future."
The Ministry of Industry, Science, Technology and Innovation is a party to implement activities related to water supply and the Ministry of Public Works and Transport is a party to implement urban sanitation activities under this project. The four target provinces of the project are Battambang, Pursat, Mondulkiri and Kandal.
"In order to maintain economic growth, Cambodia needs to help the private sector to act as a growth engine, and this can be achieved by taking immediate action," said Mariam Salim, World Bank Country Manager for Cambodia. "Improving the performance of the public sector." "Financial stability is also a priority and further efforts are needed to restore fiscal space," she added.
Going forward, economic growth may continue to be further affected by weak global demand or by rising fuel and food prices. Domestically, rising household debt and domestic credit in the real estate sector remain risks.
