Low rice prices and high rice stocks have made India the world's largest exporter over the past decade, currently accounting for nearly 40% of the total. Other African countries, such as Benin and Senegal, are major buyers in the world.
India has lifted export tariffs and set minimum prices, while white rice and non-basmati rice have been banned. Prices hit a 15-year high in August as a result of the move, with buyers from the most vulnerable importers suspending purchases. Some customers have tried to request a favor. In October, rice was still 24% higher than a year earlier, according to the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations.
A spokesman for the Indian Ministry of Commerce and Food said the Indian government was constantly monitoring food prices and making the right decisions on exports at the right time, putting the interests of consumers and farmers first. According to Bloomberg News.
Thai Prime Minister Setha Thavisin on Monday said he was "deeply concerned" about slowing economic growth in the third quarter (Video inside). More than expected in the third quarter, the economy was hit by declining exports and government spending. According to Reuters.
Citing data from the National Economic and Social Development Board (NESDC) of Thailand, Reuters reported that gross domestic product (GDP) grew 1.5 percent in the quarter from July to September. The same period last year, lower than the 2.4 percent forecast by economists in a Reuters poll.
The NESDC, the government's planning agency, said quarterly growth was the slowest in three quarters, with 1.8 percent in the second quarter and 2.6 percent in the second quarter. During the first quarter, while growth is expected to increase next year.
Strong private consumption and a booming tourism sector have helped prevent further disappointment in the growth of Southeast Asia's second-largest economy.
The prime minister said without elaborating that the Thai government was preparing further measures, including a budget package, to boost tourism.
